# Quantitative Market Research Platform — Governance Report

A quantitative market research platform that measures the predictive value of observable information under rigorous validation constraints. The trading system is one consumer of its outputs.

**Mission:** Empirically determine which aspects of financial markets are predictable with the observable information available, how much confidence that prediction deserves, and whether acquiring additional information is justified.

## Project Phase

**Active:** Phase 2 — What observable information is actually predictive?

## Progress Metrics

- ✅ **Did the platform correctly reject a weak hypothesis?** — 5 hypotheses correctly rejected
- ✅ **Did a reproducible effect survive holdout testing?** — Some holdout slices positive
- ✅ **Did research reduce uncertainty about market behavior?** — UOA: 5 hypotheses
- ✅ **Did decision quality improve on unseen data?** — Holdout distributional beats score
- ✅ **How much did we learn per unit of research effort?** — Avg efficiency 0.191; top EXP-005
- ✅ **Have unrelated L0–L1 families been tested before data investment?** — FALS-001 complete (3 families)

## Information Limit

**Can the remaining variance be explained by observable information available to us?** → **missing_information**

Exhausted explanatory power at L0–L1. Retest requires L2. Status: untestable until ingested.

## Production Alpha

**unknown_not_demonstrated** — Platform measures predictability of observable information — not demonstrated alpha

## Family A — Observable Information Verdict

| Tested | L0, L1 |
| Result | No robust edge at L0–L1 formulation after 6 hypotheses |
| Next test | L2 — Integrate OI + funding (free) before L3 microstructure |
| Status | retest_with_L2_OI_funding |

> With OHLCV and derived features (L0–L1), this liquidity continuation formulation cannot be distinguished from noise. OI/funding (L2) untested.

> Ingesting L2/L3 does not imply Family A will work. Hierarchy guides priorities, not expectations.

